```javascript
/**
 * Apply tier-based pricing to a cart and record the visit for loyalty.
 *
 * Side effects: mutates `cart` (item subtotals, total) and `user.points`.
 * Note: `cart.total` is expected to be set by the caller; this function
 * does not recompute it from the item subtotals.
 *
 * @param {{ items: Array<{ price: number, quantity: number, subtotal?: number }>, total: number }} cart
 * @param {{ tier: string, points: number }} user
 * @returns {object} The same `cart` object, mutated.
 */
function applyDiscount(cart, user) {
  // Every checkout attempt earns one loyalty point, discount or not.
  user.points += 1;

  // Populate per-line subtotals so downstream code (receipts, tax) can use them.
  for (const item of cart.items) {
    item.subtotal = item.price * item.quantity;
  }

  // TODO: reject expired coupons before applying tier discounts.
  // Gold members get 10% off the whole order.
  if (user.tier === 'gold') {
    cart.total *= 0.9;
  }

  return cart;
}
```

What changed and why:

- **Removed comments that restated the code.** "Increment loyalty points by 1" and "Loop over the items in the cart" added nothing a reader couldn't see.
- **Replaced them with intent.** The new comments say why points are awarded and who consumes the subtotals.
- **Deleted the commented-out legacy rate.** Dead code belongs in version control history, not in the function.
- **Kept the TODO but made it actionable** and placed it next to the branch it affects.
- **Added a JSDoc header** that documents the mutation side effects and the fact that the total is not recomputed from subtotals. That gap is the most likely source of bugs for a caller, so it is worth stating.
